<div class="page">
  <h1>What is a TTY?</h1>
  <p>The teletyper (or TTY) is a device that allows deaf and hearing people to type using regular phone lines. You may encounter three different terms for this device: TTY, the historical term, which is a remnant of the days when access to the telephone was first provided for Deaf individuals by the use of the Western Union Teleteypwriter, originally used for provision of telegraph service. Many Deaf people still prefer to use the term "TTY" because of its expression of heritage and ease of pronunciation, fingerspell, and speech-read. However, the more recent term, TDD (Telecommunication Device for the Deaf), which is fairly self-explanatory, is often used. A still newer term, "Text Telephone," began to appear with the passage of the Americans with Disabilities Act, but has yet to gain widespread acceptance because its application was never standardized.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; margin-top: 40px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h2>How to make a TTY call</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Place the handset of the phone in the acoustic coupler.</li>
        <li>Dial the number and wait for the tone. Two small lights will come on. Only the power light will stay on. The phone light wants to respond to any sounds picked up by the coupler.</li>
        <li>Dial the number and watch the phone light, which responds to the tone, busy signal, or ringing by corresponding light patterns. The light remains on for the length of the sound and goes off when there is not a sound. For example, the light flashes rapidly for a busy signal.</li>
        <li>People answering the phone will need TTY users with their fingers to type a short message. Followed by "GA," which means "Go ahead, your turn to talk."</li>
        <li>Start typing, making sure to identify yourself to the person answering the call.</li>
        <li>To end a turn in the conversation, type "GA" and the other person will begin typing. Each person is expected to take a turn only after receiving a "GA" from the other party.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1; margin-left: 40px;">
      <h2>TTY Etiquette</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>When calling TTY users, let the phone ring at least 10 or more times before hanging up. Many Deaf and Hard of Hearing TTY users rely on the ringing lights to alert them to ringing phones. Flashers can take longer than sound to attract attention.</li>
        <li>Users should identify themselves (i.e. Is this <span data-field="caller_name">{{ caller_name }}</span>?) at the beginning of calls.</li>
        <li>Callers should use standard abbreviations to shorten the length of the call (see list).</li>
        <li>When TTY users are calling, be sure to put on "hold" or transferred.</li>
        <li>When TTY users type, can you read me? they want to know if the message is clear and without garbled letters. If the message is clear and without garbled letters, they will type "GA." If not, when the person stops typing, you should say, "Message all garbled please, repeat if the garbled messages are not clear."</li>
        <li>If the message is not working properly or that you have a bad connection, you should say something like, "Let's hang up and you call me back or I'll call you."</li>
        <li>Punctuation marks are not commonly used during TTY conversations. Simply skip a couple of spaces between sentences.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
    <h2>How to receive a TTY call</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Usually a TTY call can be identified by a high-pitched, electronic beeping sound. However, some TTY's do not make a sound, and the phone light will flash. You may forget to tap the space bar to produce it. Therefore, if you hear no voice, assume it is a TTY call.</li>
      <li>Place the phone receiver in the acoustic coupler (for correct placement of the phone receiver, check for a graphic on the TTY of your TTY adapter and turn your TTY on).</li>
      <li>The person who answers the call is the first to type. Type your agency name and your name. Then type "GA."</li>
      <li>Continue taking turns and ending with "GA." At the end of the call, remember to type "GA to SK."</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px; text-align: center;">
    <p>Relay Service</p>
    <p>A person with a TTY can call another person without a TTY (or a campus), by calling a relay service. A relay service operator will talk to you and the other person, and will exchange messages, voice and text, between the TTY caller and the voice receiver. Anyone can reach the relay service by dialing <span data-field="relay_number">{{ relay_number }}</span>. The same type of shortcuts (i.e. "GA" and "SK") are used through relay services.</p>
  </div>
</div>
